Z / B / ALLOY
===============================================================
FORMAL METHODS 
005.13 Programming languages (formal specification)

RELATIONAL FOUNDATIONS
----------------------
Z:      Classical set theory; relations = sets of tuples.
B:      Same as Z; relational calculus + substitution rules.
Alloy:  Typed finite relational logic; everything is a relation.

Key Distinction:
Z/B = infinite mathematical universe.
Alloy = finite SAT-scope relational universe.

---------------------------------------------------------------
SCHEMA / STRUCTURING CALCULUS
------------------------------
Z:      Schema calculus (declarations + predicates).
        Supports inclusion, hiding, renaming, Δ/Ξ change schemas.

B:      Machines, variables, invariants, operations.
        No schema calculus, but operations serve similar role.

Alloy:  Signatures + facts; no schema calculus.
        Structure emerges from relational constraints.

---------------------------------------------------------------
MULTI-ARITY RELATIONS
---------------------
Z:      n-ary relations via n-tuples; projection via set comprehension.
B:      Same as Z; multi-arity exists but less emphasized.
Alloy:  First-class n-ary relations.
        Built-in operators: join, transpose, product, closure.

Key Distinction:
Alloy treats multi-arity as the *primary modeling tool*.

---------------------------------------------------------------
EXTENSIONAL EQUALITY
--------------------
Z:      Two sets/relations equal iff same members.
B:      Same classical extensionality.
Alloy:  Extensional equality enforced in SAT encoding.

Key Distinction:
Z/B = mathematical extensionality.
Alloy = computational extensionality (finite model).

---------------------------------------------------------------
CONCEPTUAL SUMMARY
------------------
Z/B:    Heavyweight, infinite, set-theoretic, structured.
Alloy:  Lightweight, finite, relational, SAT-analyzed.

All share:
- Relational semantics
- Extensional equality
- Multi-arity relations
- Formal specification intent

